%I A402109 #5 Nov 18 2026 20:30:42
%S A402109 60,72,90,140,150,168,210,252,330,350,378,390,490,510,525,570,572,588,
%T A402109 616,690,770,800,858,870,880,910,924,930,1056,1092,1110,1188,1190,
%U A402109 1230,1290,1292,1330,1386,1410,1428,1430,1456,1485,1590,1596,1610,1617,1638,1768
%N A402109 a(n) is the n-th positive integer k having two distinct composite divisors with equal sums of prime factors (with multiplicity), but no proper divisor of k has this property.
%C A402109 Call k primitive if two distinct composite divisors of k have equal prime-factor sums, but no proper divisor of k has this property. Thus this sequence consists of the minimal elements, under divisibility, among integers with such a collision.
%C A402109 Equivalently, the prime-factor multiset of k has two different sub-multisets, each of at least two elements, with equal sums, and no proper sub-multiset contains two such sub-multisets.
%C A402109 Every integer with a collision among composite divisors is divisible by a term of this sequence. Only composite divisors count; this excludes 5 and 6 in k = 30 and 13 and 22 in k = 286, since one divisor in each pair is prime.
%C A402109 Every term is the least common multiple of two distinct composite divisors of equal prime-factor sum; e.g., 60 = lcm(10, 12), 72 = lcm(8, 9), and 90 = lcm(15, 18).
%C A402109 For distinct primes p, q, p^q*q^p is a term. Also 30*p is a term for every prime p >= 7, which proves that the sequence is infinite.
%C A402109 The smallest odd term is 525 = 3*5^2*7, and the smallest squarefree term is 210 = 2*3*5*7.
%C A402109 Let c(k) count repeated prime-factor sums among composite divisors, as for A402108. For every term k, c(k) is either 1 or 2, and c(k) equals the number of unordered pairs of composite divisors having the same sum. If A001414(k) is even, then c(k) = 1; if c(k) = 2, then A001414(k) is odd and k has at least four distinct prime divisors, including three primes in arithmetic progression.
%H A402109 Felix Huber, <a href="/A402109/b402109.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A402109 Felix Huber, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24196197">Prime-Factor Sum Collisions among Composite Divisors: Primitive Cases and Additive Generalizations</a>, Zenodo (2026).
%e A402109 60 is a term: 10 and 12 are composite divisors with equal prime-factor sum 7, and no proper divisor of 60 has such a pair.
%e A402109 120 is not a term, since 60 is a proper divisor of 120 and already has a collision.
%e A402109 525 = 3*5^2*7 is a term because 21 and 25 are composite divisors with equal sums 3 + 7 = 5 + 5 = 10.
%e A402109 210 is a term with two pairs: {21, 30} has sum 10 and {35, 42} has sum 12.
%p A402109 c := proc(k)
%p A402109     local l, d, q, j, x;
%p A402109     l := [[0, 0]];
%p A402109     for q in ifactors(k)[2] do
%p A402109         l := [seq(seq([x[1] + j*q[1], x[2] + j], j = 0 .. q[2]), x in l)];
%p A402109     end do;
%p A402109     d := select(y -> y[2] > 1, l);
%p A402109     nops(d) - nops({seq(x[1], x in d)});
%p A402109 end proc:
%p A402109 select(k -> c(k) > 0 and andmap(p -> c(k/p) = 0, NumberTheory:-PrimeFactors(k)), [seq(k, k = 1 .. 1768)]);
%Y A402109 Cf. A001414, A055212, A305611, A305613, A402108.
%K A402109 nonn,new
%O A402109 1,1
%A A402109 _Felix Huber_, Nov 13 2026
